ode data-start=3D"1254" data-end=3D"1263">getName</code>) to safely modify =
and retrieve that data. The data is not directly accessible from outside th=
e class, promoting a clean interface.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"1396" data-end=3D"1438" class=3D"">2. <strong data-start=
=3D"1403" data-end=3D"1438">Abstraction (Hiding Complexity)</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"1439" data-end=3D"1562" class=3D"">In Java, classes allow =
for <strong data-start=3D"1466" data-end=3D"1481">abstraction</strong>, whe=
re you can hide the complexity of how things work behind a simple interface=
.</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1564" data-end=3D"1719">
<li data-start=3D"1564" data-end=3D"1719" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1566" data-end=3D"1594" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
566" data-end=3D"1593">Benefits of Abstraction</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1597" data-end=3D"1719">
<li data-start=3D"1597" data-end=3D"1643" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1599" data-end=3D"1643" class=3D"">Simplifies interaction =
with complex systems.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1646" data-end=3D"1719" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1648" data-end=3D"1719" class=3D"">Promotes code reusabili=
ty and modularity by hiding unnecessary details.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1721" data-end=3D"1894" class=3D"">A class can expose only=
 the necessary functionality, while keeping the inner workings hidden. For =
example, you can use an object without knowing its internal implementation:=
</p>
