art=3D"4616" data-end=3D"4621">Dog</code> is called due to polymorphism.</p=
>
<h3 data-start=3D"4654" data-end=3D"4697" class=3D"">5. <strong data-start=
=3D"4661" data-end=3D"4697">Organization and Maintainability</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"4698" data-end=3D"4892" class=3D"">Class-based design enco=
urages <strong data-start=3D"4728" data-end=3D"4742">modularity</strong>. B=
y breaking down a program into smaller, manageable components (classes), th=
e code becomes more organized, easier to test, and simpler to maintain.</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4894" data-end=3D"5163">
<li data-start=3D"4894" data-end=3D"5163" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4896" data-end=3D"4909" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"4=
896" data-end=3D"4908">Benefits</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4912" data-end=3D"5163">
<li data-start=3D"4912" data-end=3D"5048" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4914" data-end=3D"5048" class=3D"">Encourages a clean stru=
cture that mirrors real-world entities (e.g., a <code data-start=3D"4985" d=
ata-end=3D"4990">Car</code>, <code data-start=3D"4992" data-end=3D"5000">Pe=
rson</code>, or <code data-start=3D"5005" data-end=3D"5011">Book</code> can=
 each be represented as classes).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5051" data-end=3D"5163" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"5053" data-end=3D"5163" class=3D"">Promotes a clear and in=
tuitive organization of functionality, making it easier to extend, debug, a=
nd maintain.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"5165" data-end=3D"5216" class=3D"">6. <strong data-start=
=3D"5172" data-end=3D"5216">Java=E2=80=99s Object-Oriented Design Philosoph=
y</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"5217" data-end=3D"5575" class=3D"">Java=E2=80=99s class-ba=
sed approach is rooted in the philosophy of <strong data-start=3D"5276" dat=
a-end=3D"5298">object orientation</strong>, where the real world is modeled=
 through objects that have both <strong data-start=3D"5363" data-end=3D"537=
2">state</strong> (data) and <strong data-start=3D"5384" data-end=3D"5396">=
behavior</strong> (methods). Everything in Java is an object or related to =
objects, from the data types (like integers and strings) to the classes tha=
t define how objects interact with each other.</p>
<p data-start=3D"5577" data-end=3D"5613" class=3D"">This approach leads to =
code that is:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5614" data-end=3D"5856">
<li data-start=3D"5614" data-end=3D"5676" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"5616" data-end=3D"5676" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"5=
616" data-end=3D"5627">Modular</strong>: Classes can be reused and tested i=
ndependently.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5677" data-end=3D"5770" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"5679" data-end=3D"5770" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"5=
679" data-end=3D"5693">Extensible</strong>: New functionality can be added =
easily through inheritance and polymorphism.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5771" data-end=3D"5856" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"5773" data-end=3D"5856" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"5=
773" data-end=3D"5789">Maintainable</strong>: Well-structured classes lead =
to clearer and more manageable code.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"5858" data-end=3D"5870" class=3D"">Summary:</h3>
<p data-start=3D"5871" data-end=3D"5989" class=3D"">Java follows a class-ba=
sed structure because it is based on <strong data-start=3D"5931" data-end=
=3D"5962">object-oriented programming</strong> principles, which promote:</=
p>
<ul data-start=3D"5990" data-end=3D"6281">
<li data-start=3D"5990" data-end=3D"6051" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"5992" data-end=3D"6051" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"5=
992" data-end=3D"6009">Encapsulation</strong>: Bundling data and behavior i=
nto classes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6052" data-end=3D"6101" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"6054" data-end=3D"6101" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"6=
054" data-end=3D"6069">Abstraction</strong>: Hiding unnecessary complexity.=
</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6102" data-end=3D"6159" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"6104" data-end=3D"6159" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"6=
104" data-end=3D"6119">Inheritance</strong>: Reusing code and creating hier=
archies.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6160" data-end=3D"6215" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"6162" data-end=3D"6215" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"6=
162" data-end=3D"6178">Polymorphism</strong>: Writing flexible and reusable=
 code.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6216" data-end=3D"6281" class=3D"" style=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"6218" data-end=3D"6281" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"6=
218" data-end=3D"6232">Modularity</strong>: Organizing the code into manage=
able components.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"6283" data-end=3D"6490" class=3D"">These principles togeth=
er help in creating programs that are easier to develop, extend, maintain, =
and scale over time, making the class-based approach highly beneficial in l=
arge and complex software systems.</p></div></div></div></div><div class=3D=
